{
  "hostname": "BL-LT-0106",
  "reason": "Check disk space for file save failures"
}

{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Leo, sorry you've had to ask more than once. I've found the cause. Your laptop's drive is almost full, with only about 9 GB (3.5%) of space left, and that's why files won't save. You need to free up roughly 17 GB to get it working normally again. Here's the quickest way to do that:\n\n1. Empty the Recycle Bin, and delete or move anything you don't need from your Downloads folder.\n2. Go to Settings > System > Storage > Temporary files. Select the temporary files and old Windows Update files, then remove them.\n3. Turn on OneDrive Files On-Demand (OneDrive settings > Files On-Demand / \"Save space\"). Your synced files will stay in the cloud until you open them.\n4. Uninstall any apps you no longer use.\n\nWhen you're done, reply to this ticket and I'll check your free space from our side. If the drive is still too full after cleanup, I'll pass it straight to our Tier 2 team so they can look at a bigger drive or a replacement laptop. You won't need to ask again.",
  "internal_note": "User: Leo Martins (user_id 6), laptop BL-LT-0106 (Win 11 Pro, online, last check-in 2026-10-09 14:42 UTC). Disk: 9.0 GB free of 256 GB (3.5%), with a low disk space warning. This matches KB-017 ('files will not save'). I sent the user the KB-017 cleanup steps (Recycle Bin/Downloads, temp files and old WU files, OneDrive Files On-Demand, uninstall unused apps). Next step: when the user replies, re-run check_device_status. If free space is still under 10% (under ~25.6 GB), escalate to Tier 2 for a larger drive or laptop replacement as KB-017 says. User says this is their third request and is frustrated, so please prioritize the follow-up. No account issues found.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-017"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
